        {
          "id": 1952376,
          "username": "shopscout",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/s/49beb7/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-11-28T02:20:57.686Z",
          "cooked": "<p>Supplier CSVs are notorious for breaking Shopify imports. A lightweight workflow that saves a ton of manual spreadsheet cleanup is focusing on the 4 things that actually cause failed imports or messy listings:</p>\n<p>1. Multi-image row alignment: If a product has 1 variant and 5 images, make sure rows 2–5 only carry Handle, Image Src, and Image Position (leaving variant/title fields blank) so Shopify attaches all gallery photos instead of dropping them or creating duplicate variants.</p>\n<p>2. HTML &amp; link sanitization: Supplier descriptions often carry residual third-party links, inline scripts, or supplier support emails inside Body (HTML) — strip those out before importing.</p>\n<p>3. Consistent Handle &amp; Vendor normalization: Normalize handles to lowercase hyphenated slugs and overwrite the Vendor column with your own brand before upload.</p>\n<p>4. Pricing rules: Apply your markup multiplier + .99 rounding in a staging step rather than editing formulas directly inside Shopify’s export sheet (sorting rows in Excel easily detaches image rows from their handles).</p>\n<p>Full disclosure: I got tired of fixing these same supplier CSV issues by hand, so I built a free 100% browser-local utility (nothing gets uploaded to a server) that cleans Shopify CSVs, fixes multi-image rows, strips external HTML links/emails, and applies markup rounding: <a href=\"https://shopscout.cc/shopify-csv-fixer\" class=\"inline-onebox\" rel=\"noopener nofollow ugc\">Free Shopify CSV Fixer, Cleaner &amp; .99 Price Multiplier — ShopScout</a></p>\n<p>Always test on a 2–3 product slice first before importing the full catalog!</p>",
          "post_number": 5,
          "post_type": 1,
          "posts_count": 4,
          "updated_at": "2026-11-28T02:20:57.686Z",
