        {
          "id": 1952454,
          "username": "RestockSonar",
          "avatar_template": "https://avatars.discourse-cdn.com/v4/letter/r/258eb7/{size}.png",
          "created_at": "2026-11-28T06:50:53.961Z",
          "cooked": "<p>What’s worked for me with suppliers who all send different formats:</p>\n<p>Keep one master sheet with your own columns, and a small “map” tab per supplier that says which of their columns goes where. Then a new file from the same supplier is copy, paste, done, instead of starting over every time.</p>\n<p>Always keep the supplier’s own SKU in its own column, next to yours. Their codes are what you’ll match on next time they send a price list, and it’s what you put on purchase orders so they know exactly what you mean.</p>\n<p>Treat new products and updates as two different jobs. For new products you need titles, images, variants. For updates you usually only want cost and maybe barcode, and you don’t want an import quietly overwriting descriptions or stock you’ve already fixed by hand.</p>\n<p>And check the obvious stuff before importing: duplicate SKUs, empty barcodes, costs that are suddenly 10x (case price instead of unit price is the classic).</p>\n<p>Disclosure: I’m the founder of RestockSonar, a Shopify reorder and PO app. It stores each supplier’s SKU and cost per product, and you can bring those in from a CSV, but it doesn’t create new products for you, so the product import side is still the sheet.</p>",
